   <indicator aggregation-status="1" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Number of people directly supported by BIOFIN through networking and training to conserve biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">This is IKI Standard Indicator 4 (see tab &apos;SI4 | Capacity People&apos; in this workbook for the IKI-provided worksheet for reference purposes).

Definitions of sub-terms:
Direct support: Direct support is understood here as direct assistance by the project&#x2019;s training and networking measures aimed at benefitting people in their personal or professional capability to address climate change or the conservation of biodiversity. 
Off-the-job training: Off-the-job training is understood as any formal training courses developed and provided by the project that has the objective of capacity-development of its participants towards addressing climate change or the conservation of biodiversity. 
This can include training offers within technical and vocational training (TVET) or higher education, as well as project specific training offers for various target groups. The training offers can take different forms such as accredited training programmes, training of trainers, blended learning courses, repeated thematic trainings or study trips initiated and run by the project and its partners and one-off in-depth courses that last for at least half a day or longer. 

The training offer should have a formal character with a defined training concept, learning objectives and a clear scope and target group.

On-the-job training: On-the-job training is understood as continuous practical training of individuals in their workplace with the aim of sharing knowledge, skills and developing professional capacities. To this end, projects provide continuous guidance over an extended period of time through designating contact persons to individual people to be trained. These contact persons can assume mentorship roles for the people trained and, ideally, define joint learning objectives and work plans for competence and capacities development with the people trained. 
Thereby, on-the-job training is understood as going beyond informal learning that occurs in daily interactions between practitioners, civil servants, project staff or consultants within day-to-day work and work on project activities.
Networking: Support provided in the form of networking has the aim of assisting people in building their professional and personal networks to strengthen cooperation to address climate change or protect biodiversity. The support can take the form of institutionalized professional networks, the setting-up of exchange and peer-learning platforms, cross-sectoral partnerships, stakeholder meetings with the primary objectives of promoting cooperation. Ideally, it should go beyond one-off events.
Accredited training programme: An accredited training programme is understood as a program that leads to a formal qualification of an individual such as an advanced diploma, degree or certificate that is recognized beyond the training organization in a distinct professional field or on the national level. 
Typical examples included university degrees, formal technical and vocational education and training (TVET) and recognized professional qualifications.</narrative>
